  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "The current density in a cylindrical wire of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The current through the outer portion of the wire between radial distances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "48",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Current density is defined as current per unit cross-section area. Therefore,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>48</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p>",
